cade clears 2.50% mainly on 2027-2030, not on 2032-2036. P(growth < 2.0%) from CQ3 = P(z <= 0.233) = 0.59. Correlation +0.35 (a supply-wall world is both hot and slow): 0.27 x 0.59 x 1.35 = 0.215 -> C = 21. A and C are disjoint on growth, so no lexical adjustment needed.\n\nStep 3 (D = growth <1.7% AND core <2.0%): P(growth < 1.7%) = 0.45; P(core 2032-36 < 2.0%) = 0.30 — held DOWN relative to a naive AI-deflation view by the basket weights, because OECD core is shelter- and administered-services-heavy (shelter ~30-33% of US core CPI, ~17-18% of core PCE) and AI deflates software, media and professional services, which carry only ~0.20-0.25 of the basket. Correlation +0.40 (one demand-deficit regime): 0.45 x 0.30 x 1.45 = 0.196 -> D = 20.\n\nStep 4 (B = residual): 100 - 17 - 21 - 20 = 42. B is modal, which is the direct implication of my relative-price-skew framing — electricity, construction and care inflating at 4-6% while software, media, professional services and tradable goods run 0-1% produces a MODERATE core aggregate with EXTREME dispersion, i.e. the barbell, plus steep real term premia consistent with my CQ4 P50 of 2.1% against a 2.0% current level. It also brackets the standalone AI-Q6 Jagged Diffusion read of 47.2% from below, the gap being the mass that coupling moves into C and D.\nSum = 17 + 42 + 21 + 20 = 100.\nCross-check against CQ2: my CQ2 of 34 requires a hot 2027-2036 OECD decade WITH Chinese CPI under 1.5%. That is a B-or-C world, and B+C = 63 in my distribution, comfortably above 34 — no contradiction. If instead I had put heavy mass on D (core <2.0% in 2032-36), CQ2 at 34 would have been hard to sustain; D at 20 is the amount of deflationary mass my CQ2 can carry.","referenceClassIds":[],"schemaVersion":"forecast-workbench.forecast/v1","sealedAt":"2026-11-04T17:31:56.805Z","seriesId":"published-panel","strongestContraryCase":"Not elicited per question. Panelist falsification signposts: {\"signpost\":\"US core PCE 12-month rate for December 2028